Terminal operations.

An intermediate chain is a recipe that never cooks. The terminal operation is the one that runs it. It consumes the stream, drives every element through the pipeline, and produces a result that is not a stream — a value, a collection, a boolean, or a side effect. Exactly one terminal ends every pipeline, and attaching it is what makes anything happen at all.

Here's the defining trait, and it has two sides of the same coin. First, a terminal is eager — the moment it's called, the whole pipeline executes. Second, it consumes the stream — once a terminal has run, that stream is closed. Touch it again, and you get an illegal-state exception: the stream has already been operated upon or closed. A stream is a one-shot. For a second pass, you start a fresh one from the source. So if you take a stream, call count on it, and then try to call for-each on the same stream, that second call throws — the stream is already spent.

Now let's group the terminals by what they hand back. The first family reduces to a single value — collapsing the whole stream to one answer. Count gives you a long, how many elements. On a primitive stream, sum adds them up. Average gives you an optional-double — because the stream might be empty. Min and max, with a comparator, give you an optional of the element type. And notice that average, min, and max all return an optional: an empty stream has no minimum, so the type honestly says "maybe". That's section nine.

The second family is search — and these short-circuit. They ask a question and stop as soon as it's answered. Any-match returns true the instant one element passes; the rest are never examined. All-match returns false the instant one fails. There's also none-match. Find-first returns an optional of the first element, then stops. And find-any returns an optional of any element, which is cheaper in parallel. These are the real payoff of laziness: on an infinite or huge stream, find-first pulls just enough elements to answer, and then halts.

The third family collects into a container — gathering the elements into a data structure. To-list, from Java sixteen on, is the modern shorthand for a list. Collect with collectors-to-set gives you a set. And collect with collectors is a whole world of its own — grouping, joining, summarizing — which gets the entire next section.

The fourth family performs a side effect — doing something with each element and returning nothing. For-each prints or writes each element and returns void. It's the stream's exit into the imperative world. Reach for it only for genuine side effects, like printing or writing. If you're building a value, use a collect or a reduction — not a for-each that mutates some external variable.

Let me close on a recurring theme: why terminals return optional instead of null. Find-first, min, max, the one-argument reduce, average — all of them return an optional rather than a bare value or a null. The reason is honesty about emptiness. Filter a stream down to nothing, and there is no first element. The type should force you to handle that case, rather than hand you a null that explodes three lines later. That deliberate pairing — pipeline in, optional out — is exactly why sections nine and ten close this module on optional.
